# ⚠ ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH. These come from the standard Python template, where
# they mean the TOP-LEVEL setuptools build directories. Unanchored, `lib/` also matches
# `scripts/lib/` — a first-class source directory with ten tracked modules — so every
# NEW file added there was silently skipped by `git add`, with no message and no entry
# in `git status`. Measured twice on 2026-08-17: `scripts/lib/find_changeset.py` existed
# in no commit at all, and `scripts/lib/schemas.py` was invisible to a guard that
# enumerates via `git ls-files`. Existing files stayed tracked, which is what kept it
# hidden: the rule only ever ate the next one.
#
# ⚠ THE TEMPLATE NAMES ARE ANCHORED; A SHORT LIST IS DELIBERATELY NOT (issue #98).
# Fixing `lib/` and `lib64/` alone treated a general defect as two incidents — `build/`,
# `dist/`, `parts/`, `var/`, `wheels/`, `env/`, `venv/`, `logs`, `outputs` and the rest are
# the same unanchored shape from the same Python template, and `scripts/build/` or
# `matcha/var/` would be eaten exactly as `scripts/lib/` was: silently, absent from
# `git status`. The two that bit were not special; they were only first.
#
# ⚠ AND THE FIRST FIX CLAIMED MORE THAN IT DID. Its comment said "EVERY DIRECTORY NAME FROM
# THAT TEMPLATE IS NOW ANCHORED" while `dist/` sat two lines above `/build/`, still bare.
# A false claim written into a comment while fixing a false-claim issue — which is why the
# rule is now enforced by `tests/test_gitignore_anchoring.py` rather than stated here.
#
# RECURSIVE BY DESIGN, and the test knows their names: `__pycache__/` and the build-artifact
# directories (`model_tflite/`, `synth_output/`, …) SHOULD match at any depth. Anchoring them
# would be the opposite error. Adding to that list is a decision made in the test, not a
# slip made here.
/lib/
/lib64/
/parts/
/sdist/
/var/
/wheels/
/share/python-wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

